  "question": {
    "text": "If $f(x)$ is a function such that $f^{\\prime}(x)=(x-1)^{2}(4-x),$ then"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$f(0)=0$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$f(x)$ is increasing in (0,3)",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$x=4$ is a critical point of $f(x)$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$f(x)$ is decreasing in (3,5)",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B",
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given\r\n$$\r\nf^{\\prime}(x)=(x-1)^{2}(4-x)\r\n$$\r\nThe sign scheme of $f^{\\prime}(x)$\r\n<img src=\"assets\\st0056_84fa0ad9ec50.png\">\r\n\r\nClearly. $f(x)$ is increasing, for $x \\in(-\\infty, 4)$ and decreasing, for $n \\in(4, \\infty)$ \r\nSince, $f^{\\prime}(x)=0$ at $x=4$\r\n$\\mathrm{SO}, \\mathrm{x}=4$ is a critical point."
  }
